(646,18,13,'Type conversion','**Type conversion** changes a **value** from one **type** to another. Text must become a number before you can do math with it.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nstring t = "42";\nint n = int.Parse(t);\nConsole.WriteLine(n + 8);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n50\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nint n = 7;\nstring s = (n).ToString();\nConsole.WriteLine(s + " days");\n```\nOutput:\n```\n7 days\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\nstring t = "8";\nint n = int.Parse(t);\nConsole.WriteLine(n + 10);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n18\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(647,18,14,'Constants','A **constant** is a value that never changes, such as a tax rate. Write it in capital letters so it is easy to spot.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nconst int MAX = 5;\nConsole.WriteLine("" + ("Max is ") + (MAX));\n```\nOutput:\n```\nMax is 5\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nconst double PI = 3.14;\nint r = 2;\nConsole.WriteLine(PI * r * r);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n12.56\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(648,18,15,'Comments','A **comment** is a note for people. The computer **ignores** it. Use comments to explain what your code does.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n// This line is a comment\nConsole.WriteLine("Hello");\n// Comments help people read code\n```\nOutput:\n```\nHello\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n// Calculate the total\nint price = 20;\nint qty = 3;\nConsole.WriteLine(price * qty);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n60\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(649,18,16,'Naming rules','Give every **variable** a clear **name**. Use letters, digits and underscores, and never start with a digit. Capital letters matter.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nint studentAge = 17;\nint total_score = 90;\nConsole.WriteLine(studentAge + total_score);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n107\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nstring firstName = "Ana";\nConsole.WriteLine("" + ("Hello ") + (firstName));\n```\nOutput:\n```\nHello Ana\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\nint studentAge = 7;\nint total_score = 5;\nConsole.WriteLine(studentAge + total_score);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n12\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(650,18,17,'Booleans','A **boolean** is either **true** or **false**. Use it for yes or no answers and for **conditions**.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nbool isOpen = true;\nif (isOpen) {\n    Console.WriteLine("Open");\n} else {\n    Console.WriteLine("Closed");\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\nOpen\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nint age = 20;\nbool adult = age >= 18;\nif (adult) {\n    Console.WriteLine("Adult");\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\nAdult\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(651,18,18,'Increment and decrement','**++** adds 1 to a variable and **--** subtracts 1. They are handy for counting.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nint n = 5;\nn++;\nConsole.WriteLine(n);\nn--;\nConsole.WriteLine(n);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n6\n5\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nint count = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n    count++;\n}\nConsole.WriteLine(count);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n4\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(652,18,19,'Compound assignment','**Compound assignment** changes a variable using its own value: **+=**, **-=**, ***=** and **/=**.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nint score = 10;\nscore += 5;\nConsole.WriteLine(score);\nscore *= 2;\nConsole.WriteLine(score);\nscore -= 3;\nConsole.WriteLine(score);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n15\n30\n27\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nint x = 20;\nx /= 4;\nConsole.WriteLine(x);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n5\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\nint score = 10;\nscore += 8;\nConsole.WriteLine(score);\nscore *= 2;\nConsole.WriteLine(score);\nscore -= 3;\nConsole.WriteLine(score);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n18\n36\n33\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(653,18,20,'Operator precedence','**Precedence** decides which **operator** runs first. Multiplication and division come before addition and subtraction. Use **brackets** to choose the order yourself.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nConsole.WriteLine(2 + 3 * 4);\nConsole.WriteLine((2 + 3) * 4);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n14\n20\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nConsole.WriteLine(10 - 4 - 3);\nConsole.WriteLine(10 - (4 - 3));\n```\nOutput:\n```\n3\n9\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\nConsole.WriteLine(2 + 12 * 4);\nConsole.WriteLine((2 + 12) * 4);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n50\n56\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(654,18,21,'Nested if','A **nested if** is an if inside another if. The inner check only happens when the outer one is true.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nint age = 20;\nbool hasId = true;\nif (age >= 18) {\n    if (hasId) {\n        Console.WriteLine("Welcome");\n    } else {\n        Console.WriteLine("Show your ID");\n    }\n} else {\n    Console.WriteLine("Too young");\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\nWelcome\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nint score = 85;\nif (score >= 50) {\n    if (score >= 80) {\n        Console.WriteLine("Great");\n    } else {\n        Console.WriteLine("Pass");\n    }\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\nGreat\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(655,18,22,'switch statements','A **switch** picks one **case** by matching a value. **break** stops it from running into the next case, and **default** handles everything else.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nint day = 3;\nswitch (day) {\n    case 1:\n        Console.WriteLine("Monday");\n        break;\n    case 3:\n        Console.WriteLine("Wednesday");\n        break;\n    default:\n        Console.WriteLine("Another day");\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\nWednesday\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nint n = 2;\nswitch (n) {\n    case 1:\n    case 2:\n        Console.WriteLine("Small");\n        break;\n    default:\n        Console.WriteLine("Big");\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\nSmall\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(656,18,23,'Ternary operator','The **ternary operator** is a short if: **condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse**.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nint age = 17;\nstring label = (age >= 18) ? "adult" : "minor";\nConsole.WriteLine(label);\n```\nOutput:\n```\nminor\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nint a = 8;\nint b = 5;\nint big = (a > b) ? a : b;\nConsole.WriteLine(big);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n8\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(657,18,24,'do while loops','A **do while** loop runs its body **first** and checks the condition **after**, so it always runs at least once.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nint n = 1;\ndo {\n    Console.WriteLine(n);\n    n++;\n} while (n <= 3);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n1\n2\n3\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nint n = 10;\ndo {\n    Console.WriteLine("Runs once");\n    n++;\n} while (n < 5);\n```\nOutput:\n```\nRuns once\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\nint n = 1;\ndo {\n    Console.WriteLine(n);\n    n++;\n} while (n <= 5);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n1\n2\n3\n4\n5\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(658,18,25,'break and continue','**break** leaves a loop right away. **continue** skips the rest of this round and goes to the next one.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nfor (int i = 1; i <= 6; i++) {\n    if (i == 3) {\n        continue;\n    }\n    if (i == 5) {\n        break;\n    }\n    Console.WriteLine(i);\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n1\n2\n4\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nint i = 0;\nwhile (i < 10) {\n    i += 3;\n    if (i > 7) {\n        break;\n    }\n    Console.WriteLine(i);\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n3\n6\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\nfor (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {\n    if (i == 10) {\n        continue;\n    }\n    if (i == 9) {\n        break;\n    }\n    Console.WriteLine(i);\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n1\n2\n3\n4\n5\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(659,18,26,'Nested loops','A **nested loop** is a loop inside a loop. The inner loop runs completely for every round of the outer loop.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nfor (int r = 1; r <= 3; r++) {\n    for (int c = 1; c <= 3; c++) {\n        Console.WriteLine(r * c);\n    }\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n1\n2\n3\n2\n4\n6\n3\n6\n9\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nfor (int i = 1; i <= 4; i++) {\n    string line = "";\n    for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {\n        line = line + "*";\n    }\n    Console.WriteLine(line);\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n*\n**\n***\n****\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\nfor (int r = 1; r <= 5; r++) {\n    for (int c = 1; c <= 5; c++) {\n        Console.WriteLine(r * c);\n    }\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n1\n2\n3\n4\n5\n2\n4\n6\n8\n10\n3\n6\n9\n12\n15\n4\n8\n12\n16\n20\n5\n10\n15\n20\n25\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(660,18,27,'Counting with loops','Use a **counter** variable that starts at 0 and goes up by 1 each time something matches.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nint count = 0;\nfor (int i = 1; i <= 20; i++) {\n    if (i % 3 == 0) {\n        count++;\n    }\n}\nConsole.WriteLine(count);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n6\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nstring word = "banana";\nint a = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < word.Length; i++) {\n    if (word[i] == \'a\') {\n        a++;\n    }\n}\nConsole.WriteLine(a);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n3\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\nint count = 0;\nfor (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {\n    if (i % 6 == 0) {\n        count++;\n    }\n}\nConsole.WriteLine(count);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n0\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(661,18,28,'Accumulator pattern','An **accumulator** is a variable that starts at 0 and **adds** to itself each round to build a total.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nint total = 0;\nfor (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {\n    total += i;\n}\nConsole.WriteLine(total);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n15\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nvar prices = new List<int> { 5, 10, 20 };\nint sum = 0;\nforeach (int p in prices) {\n    sum += p;\n}\nConsole.WriteLine("" + ("Total: ") + (sum));\n```\nOutput:\n```\nTotal: 35\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\nint total = 0;\nfor (int i = 1; i <= 4; i++) {\n    total += i;\n}\nConsole.WriteLine(total);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n10\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.');
INSERT INTO `new_lessons`(id,module_id,position,title,body) VALUES
(662,18,29,'Arrays basics','An **array** (or **list**) keeps many values under one name. Each value has a **position** called an **index**, and the first index is **0**.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nvar v = new List<int> { 4, 9, 2 };\nConsole.WriteLine(v[0]);\nConsole.WriteLine(v[2]);\nConsole.WriteLine(v.Count);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n4\n2\n3\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nvar scores = new List<int> { 70, 85, 90 };\nforeach (int s in scores) {\n    Console.WriteLine(s);\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n70\n85\n90\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\nvar v = new List<int> { 5, 11, 2 };\nConsole.WriteLine(v[0]);\nConsole.WriteLine(v[2]);\nConsole.WriteLine(v.Count);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n5\n2\n3\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(663,18,30,'Looping arrays','Use a **for** loop with the **index** to visit each item, or a loop that gives you each item directly.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nvar v = new List<int> { 3, 6, 9 };\nfor (int i = 0; i < v.Count; i++) {\n    Console.WriteLine("" + (i) + (": ") + (v[i]));\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n0: 3\n1: 6\n2: 9\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nvar v = new List<int> { 2, 4, 6 };\nint sum = 0;\nforeach (int x in v) {\n    sum += x;\n}\nConsole.WriteLine(sum);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n12\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\nvar v = new List<int> { 12, 4, 9 };\nfor (int i = 0; i < v.Count; i++) {\n    Console.WriteLine("" + (i) + (": ") + (v[i]));\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n0: 12\n1: 4\n2: 9\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(664,18,31,'Array length','The **length** tells you how many items are in an array. The **last** index is always the length **minus 1**.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nvar v = new List<int> { 5, 6, 7, 8 };\nConsole.WriteLine(v.Count);\nConsole.WriteLine(v[v.Count - 1]);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n4\n8\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nstring s = "hello";\nConsole.WriteLine(s.Length);\nConsole.WriteLine(s[s.Length - 1]);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n5\no\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(665,18,32,'Searching an array','To **search**, check every item and remember if you found what you want, or remember its **position**.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nvar v = new List<int> { 7, 3, 9, 4 };\nint found = 0;\nforeach (int x in v) {\n    if (x == 9) {\n        found = 1;\n    }\n}\nif (found == 1) {\n    Console.WriteLine("Found it");\n} else {\n    Console.WriteLine("Not found");\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\nFound it\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nvar v = new List<int> { 7, 3, 9, 4 };\nint pos = -1;\nfor (int i = 0; i < v.Count; i++) {\n    if (v[i] == 3) {\n        pos = i;\n    }\n}\nConsole.WriteLine(pos);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n1\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(666,18,33,'Finding min and max','Start with the **first** item as the biggest (or smallest) and **replace** it whenever you see a better one.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nvar v = new List<int> { 8, 2, 9, 4 };\nint big = v[0];\nint small = v[0];\nforeach (int x in v) {\n    if (x > big) {\n        big = x;\n    }\n    if (x < small) {\n        small = x;\n    }\n}\nConsole.WriteLine("" + ("Biggest: ") + (big));\nConsole.WriteLine("" + ("Smallest: ") + (small));\n```\nOutput:\n```\nBiggest: 9\nSmallest: 2\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nvar temps = new List<int> { 30, 28, 33 };\nint hottest = temps[0];\nforeach (int t in temps) {\n    if (t > hottest) {\n        hottest = t;\n    }\n}\nConsole.WriteLine(hottest);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n33\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(667,18,34,'String methods','A **string** is text. It has a **length**, and **methods** (built-in tools) such as taking a piece of it.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nstring s = "Hello World";\nConsole.WriteLine(s.Length);\nConsole.WriteLine(s.Substring(0, 5));\n```\nOutput:\n```\n11\nHello\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nstring a = "code";\nstring b = "smart";\nstring c = a + b;\nConsole.WriteLine(c);\nConsole.WriteLine(c.Length);\n```\nOutput:\n```\ncodesmart\n9\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\nstring s = "Hello World";\nConsole.WriteLine(s.Length);\nConsole.WriteLine(s.Substring(0, 7));\n```\nOutput:\n```\n11\nHello W\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(668,18,35,'Substrings','A **substring** is a piece of a string. You give the **start** position and how many characters to take.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nstring s = "programming";\nConsole.WriteLine(s.Substring(0, 3));\n```\nOutput:\n```\npro\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nstring s = "programming";\nConsole.WriteLine(s.Substring(3, 4));\n```\nOutput:\n```\ngram\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\nstring s = "programming";\nConsole.WriteLine(s.Substring(0, 8));\n```\nOutput:\n```\nprogramm\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(669,18,36,'Searching text','You can **search** inside a string to see if a word is there and **where** it starts.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nstring s = "the cat sat";\nif (s.Contains("cat")) {\n    Console.WriteLine("Found cat");\n} else {\n    Console.WriteLine("No cat");\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\nFound cat\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nstring s = "the cat sat";\nConsole.WriteLine(s.IndexOf("sat"));\n```\nOutput:\n```\n8\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(670,18,37,'Formatting output','Join text and values in one line to make clear **output**. An empty print makes a **blank line**.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nstring name = "Ana";\nint score = 95;\nConsole.WriteLine("" + (name) + (" scored ") + (score) + (" points"));\n```\nOutput:\n```\nAna scored 95 points\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nConsole.WriteLine("Line 1");\nConsole.WriteLine("");\nConsole.WriteLine("Line 3");\n```\nOutput:\n```\nLine 1\n\nLine 3\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\nstring name = "Ana";\nint score = 3;\nConsole.WriteLine("" + (name) + (" scored ") + (score) + (" points"));\n```\nOutput:\n```\nAna scored 3 points\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(671,18,38,'Function parameters','**Parameters** are the inputs of a **function**. The values you pass in when you call it are called **arguments**.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nint add(int a, int b) {\n    return a + b;\n}\n\nConsole.WriteLine(add(3, 4));\nConsole.WriteLine(add(10, 20));\n```\nOutput:\n```\n7\n30\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nint area(int w, int h) {\n    return w * h;\n}\n\nConsole.WriteLine("" + ("Area: ") + (area(4, 5)));\n```\nOutput:\n```\nArea: 20\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\nint add(int a, int b) {\n    return a + b;\n}\n\nConsole.WriteLine(add(6, 3));\nConsole.WriteLine(add(9, 11));\n```\nOutput:\n```\n9\n20\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(672,18,39,'Return values','**return** sends a **value** back to the place where the function was called. You can store it or use it right away.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nint square(int n) {\n    return n * n;\n}\n\nint r = square(6);\nConsole.WriteLine(r);\nConsole.WriteLine(square(3) + 1);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n36\n10\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nint bigger(int a, int b) {\n    if (a > b) {\n        return a;\n    }\n    return b;\n}\n\nConsole.WriteLine(bigger(7, 12));\n```\nOutput:\n```\n12\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\nint square(int n) {\n    return n * n;\n}\n\nint r = square(10);\nConsole.WriteLine(r);\nConsole.WriteLine(square(9) + 1);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n100\n82\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(673,18,40,'Default parameters','A **default parameter** has a value that is used when the caller does not give one.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nint power(int base, int times = 2) {\n    int r = 1;\n    for (int i = 0; i < times; i++) {\n        r = r * base;\n    }\n    return r;\n}\n\nConsole.WriteLine(power(5));\nConsole.WriteLine(power(2, 5));\n```\nOutput:\n```\n25\n32\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nstring greet(string name = "friend") {\n    return "Hello " + name;\n}\n\nConsole.WriteLine(greet());\nConsole.WriteLine(greet("Ana"));\n```\nOutput:\n```\nHello friend\nHello Ana\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(674,18,41,'Function overview','A **function** is a named block of code you can **call** again and again. It keeps your program short and tidy.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nvoid sayHi() {\n    Console.WriteLine("Hi!");\n}\n\nsayHi();\nsayHi();\n```\nOutput:\n```\nHi!\nHi!\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nvoid line() {\n    Console.WriteLine("----------");\n}\n\nline();\nConsole.WriteLine("Menu");\nline();\n```\nOutput:\n```\n----------\nMenu\n----------\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(675,18,42,'Variable scope','**Scope** is where a variable can be used. A variable made inside a function or loop only exists **there**.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nvoid show() {\n    int inner = 5;\n    Console.WriteLine(inner);\n}\n\nint outer = 1;\nshow();\nConsole.WriteLine(outer);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n5\n1\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nfor (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {\n    int temp = i * 10;\n    Console.WriteLine(temp);\n}\nConsole.WriteLine("Done");\n```\nOutput:\n```\n0\n10\nDone\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\nvoid show() {\n    int inner = 3;\n    Console.WriteLine(inner);\n}\n\nint outer = 1;\nshow();\nConsole.WriteLine(outer);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n3\n1\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(676,18,43,'Recursion intro','**Recursion** means a function **calls itself**. It needs a **base case** that stops it.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nint countdownSum(int n) {\n    if (n == 0) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    return n + countdownSum(n - 1);\n}\n\nConsole.WriteLine(countdownSum(4));\n```\nOutput:\n```\n10\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nint fact(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) {\n        return 1;\n    }\n    return n * fact(n - 1);\n}\n\nConsole.WriteLine(fact(5));\n```\nOutput:\n```\n120\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\nint countdownSum(int n) {\n    if (n == 0) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    return n + countdownSum(n - 1);\n}\n\nConsole.WriteLine(countdownSum(6));\n```\nOutput:\n```\n21\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(677,18,44,'Simple calculator','A **calculator** uses the math **operators** on two numbers. Whole-number division drops the decimals.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nint a = 12;\nint b = 4;\nConsole.WriteLine(a + b);\nConsole.WriteLine(a - b);\nConsole.WriteLine(a * b);\nConsole.WriteLine((a / b));\n```\nOutput:\n```\n16\n8\n48\n3\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nint x = 9;\nint y = 3;\nint op = 3;\nswitch (op) {\n    case 1:\n        Console.WriteLine(x + y);\n        break;\n    case 3:\n        Console.WriteLine(x * y);\n        break;\n    default:\n        Console.WriteLine("Unknown");\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n27\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\nint a = 4;\nint b = 9;\nConsole.WriteLine(a + b);\nConsole.WriteLine(a - b);\nConsole.WriteLine(a * b);\nConsole.WriteLine((a / b));\n```\nOutput:\n```\n13\n-5\n36\n0\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(678,18,45,'Number guessing logic','A guessing game **compares** the guess with a secret number and tells the player **too low**, **too high** or **correct**.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nint secret = 7;\nint guess = 4;\nif (guess == secret) {\n    Console.WriteLine("Correct");\n} else if (guess < secret) {\n    Console.WriteLine("Too low");\n} else {\n    Console.WriteLine("Too high");\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\nToo low\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nvar guesses = new List<int> { 3, 9, 7 };\nint secret = 7;\nforeach (int g in guesses) {\n    if (g == secret) {\n        Console.WriteLine("" + ("Got it: ") + (g));\n    } else {\n        Console.WriteLine("Try again");\n    }\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\nTry again\nTry again\nGot it: 7\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\nint secret = 3;\nint guess = 12;\nif (guess == secret) {\n    Console.WriteLine("Correct");\n} else if (guess < secret) {\n    Console.WriteLine("Too low");\n} else {\n    Console.WriteLine("Too high");\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\nToo high\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(679,18,46,'Temperature converter','To change **Celsius** to **Fahrenheit**, multiply by 9, divide by 5 and add 32.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nint c = 20;\nint f = c * 9 / 5 + 32;\nConsole.WriteLine(f);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n68\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nfor (int c = 0; c <= 40; c += 20) {\n    Console.WriteLine("" + (c) + (" C = ") + (c * 9 / 5 + 32) + (" F"));\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n0 C = 32 F\n20 C = 68 F\n40 C = 104 F\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(680,18,47,'Grade calculator','A **grade calculator** uses **if / else if** to turn a score into a letter. Check the highest score first.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nint score = 86;\nif (score >= 90) {\n    Console.WriteLine("A");\n} else if (score >= 80) {\n    Console.WriteLine("B");\n} else if (score >= 70) {\n    Console.WriteLine("C");\n} else {\n    Console.WriteLine("F");\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\nB\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nvar marks = new List<int> { 80, 90, 70 };\nint sum = 0;\nforeach (int m in marks) {\n    sum += m;\n}\nConsole.WriteLine("" + ("Average: ") + ((sum / marks.Count)));\n```\nOutput:\n```\nAverage: 80\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(681,18,48,'Reading error messages','An **error message** tells you what went wrong and **which line**. Read the first message first, fix it, then run again. Typical errors are a **missing semicolon**, a **misspelled name** and **mixed types**.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nint count = 3;\nConsole.WriteLine("" + ("Count is ") + (count));\n// A missing semicolon on the line above would give an error\n```\nOutput:\n```\nCount is 3\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nstring name = "Ana";\nConsole.WriteLine("" + ("Hello ") + (name));\n// Writing nam instead of name would give an unknown name error\n```\nOutput:\n```\nHello Ana\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(682,18,49,'Debugging basics','**Debugging** means finding and fixing mistakes. A great trick is to **print** the values while the program runs.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nint total = 0;\nfor (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {\n    total += i;\n    Console.WriteLine("" + ("i = ") + (i) + (" total = ") + (total));\n}\nConsole.WriteLine(total);\n```\nOutput:\n```\ni = 1 total = 1\ni = 2 total = 3\ni = 3 total = 6\n6\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nint a = 6;\nint b = 4;\nConsole.WriteLine("" + ("a is ") + (a));\nConsole.WriteLine("" + ("b is ") + (b));\nConsole.WriteLine("" + ("a - b is ") + (a - b));\n```\nOutput:\n```\na is 6\nb is 4\na - b is 2\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\nint total = 0;\nfor (int i = 1; i <= 4; i++) {\n    total += i;\n    Console.WriteLine("" + ("i = ") + (i) + (" total = ") + (total));\n}\nConsole.WriteLine(total);\n```\nOutput:\n```\ni = 1 total = 1\ni = 2 total = 3\ni = 3 total = 6\ni = 4 total = 10\n10\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(683,18,50,'Mini project','Time to combine what you learned: **variables**, **loops**, **if** and **arrays** in small programs.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n// Mini project: multiplication table\nint n = 4;\nfor (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {\n    Console.WriteLine("" + (n) + (" x ") + (i) + (" = ") + (n * i));\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n4 x 1 = 4\n4 x 2 = 8\n4 x 3 = 12\n4 x 4 = 16\n4 x 5 = 20\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nvar v = new List<int> { 5, 12, 8 };\nint sum = 0;\nint big = v[0];\nforeach (int x in v) {\n    sum += x;\n    if (x > big) {\n        big = x;\n    }\n}\nConsole.WriteLine("" + ("Sum: ") + (sum));\nConsole.WriteLine("" + ("Biggest: ") + (big));\n```\nOutput:\n```\nSum: 25\nBiggest: 12\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\n// Mini project: multiplication table\nint n = 12;\nfor (int i = 1; i <= 6; i++) {\n    Console.WriteLine("" + (n) + (" x ") + (i) + (" = ") + (n * i));\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n12 x 1 = 12\n12 x 2 = 24\n12 x 3 = 36\n12 x 4 = 48\n12 x 5 = 60\n12 x 6 = 72\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(684,23,1,'Functions in depth','A **function** can take several **parameters**, return a **value** and call other functions. Split a big problem into small functions.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nint square(int n) {\n    return n * n;\n}\nint sumOfSquares(int a, int b) {\n    return square(a) + square(b);\n}\n\nConsole.WriteLine(sumOfSquares(3, 4));\n```\nOutput:\n```\n25\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nint isEven(int n) {\n    if (n % 2 == 0) {\n        return 1;\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n\nConsole.WriteLine(isEven(8));\nConsole.WriteLine(isEven(7));\n```\nOutput:\n```\n1\n0\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\nint square(int n) {\n    return n * n;\n}\nint sumOfSquares(int a, int b) {\n    return square(a) + square(b);\n}\n\nConsole.WriteLine(sumOfSquares(6, 8));\n```\nOutput:\n```\n100\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(685,23,2,'ref and out parameters',NULL),
(686,23,3,'Recursion','**Recursion** solves a big problem by solving a smaller copy of it. Always write the **base case** first.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nint power(int b, int e) {\n    if (e == 0) {\n        return 1;\n    }\n    return b * power(b, e - 1);\n}\n\nConsole.WriteLine(power(2, 5));\n```\nOutput:\n```\n32\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nint sumDigits(int n) {\n    if (n < 10) {\n        return n;\n    }\n    return n % 10 + sumDigits((n / 10));\n}\n\nConsole.WriteLine(sumDigits(1234));\n```\nOutput:\n```\n10\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\nint power(int b, int e) {\n    if (e == 0) {\n        return 1;\n    }\n    return b * power(b, e - 1);\n}\n\nConsole.WriteLine(power(2, 3));\n```\nOutput:\n```\n8\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(687,23,4,'Scope in depth',NULL),
(688,23,5,'Strings in depth','You can walk through a **string** one **character** at a time with its **index**, to reverse it or count things.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nstring s = "hello";\nstring r = "";\nfor (int i = 0; i < s.Length; i++) {\n    r = s[i] + r;\n}\nConsole.WriteLine(r);\n```\nOutput:\n```\nolleh\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nstring s = "education";\nint c = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < s.Length; i++) {\n    if (s[i] == \'a\' || s[i] == \'e\' || s[i] == \'i\' || s[i] == \'o\' || s[i] == \'u\') {\n        c++;\n    }\n}\nConsole.WriteLine(c);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n5\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
