(690,23,7,'Splitting text',NULL),
(691,23,8,'Searching and replacing',NULL),
(692,23,9,'Arrays in depth','You can **change** the items of an array while you loop over it, and use **nested** loops to compare items.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nvar v = new List<int> { 1, 2, 3, 4 };\nfor (int i = 0; i < v.Count; i++) {\n    v[i] = v[i] * 2;\n}\nforeach (int x in v) {\n    Console.WriteLine(x);\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n2\n4\n6\n8\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nvar v = new List<int> { 4, 7, 4, 9 };\nint dups = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < v.Count; i++) {\n    for (int j = i + 1; j < v.Count; j++) {\n        if (v[i] == v[j]) {\n            dups++;\n        }\n    }\n}\nConsole.WriteLine(dups);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n1\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\nvar v = new List<int> { 1, 2, 4, 5 };\nfor (int i = 0; i < v.Count; i++) {\n    v[i] = v[i] * 2;\n}\nforeach (int x in v) {\n    Console.WriteLine(x);\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n2\n4\n8\n10\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(693,23,10,'Two-dimensional arrays',NULL),
(694,23,11,'Sorting a list','**Sorting** puts items in order. **Bubble sort** compares neighbors and swaps them when they are in the wrong order.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nvar v = new List<int> { 9, 3, 7, 1 };\nint n = v.Count;\nfor (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {\n    for (int j = 0; j < n - 1 - i; j++) {\n        if (v[j] > v[j + 1]) {\n            int t = v[j];\n            v[j] = v[j + 1];\n            v[j + 1] = t;\n        }\n    }\n}\nforeach (int x in v) {\n    Console.WriteLine(x);\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n1\n3\n7\n9\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nvar v = new List<int> { 4, 8, 2, 6 };\nint n = v.Count;\nfor (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {\n    for (int j = 0; j < n - 1 - i; j++) {\n        if (v[j] < v[j + 1]) {\n            int t = v[j];\n            v[j] = v[j + 1];\n            v[j + 1] = t;\n        }\n    }\n}\nforeach (int x in v) {\n    Console.WriteLine(x);\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n8\n6\n4\n2\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\nvar v = new List<int> { 4, 11, 3, 1 };\nint n = v.Count;\nfor (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {\n    for (int j = 0; j < n - 1 - i; j++) {\n        if (v[j] > v[j + 1]) {\n            int t = v[j];\n            v[j] = v[j + 1];\n            v[j + 1] = t;\n        }\n    }\n}\nforeach (int x in v) {\n    Console.WriteLine(x);\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n1\n3\n4\n11\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(695,23,12,'Searching a list','**Linear search** checks the items one by one from the start until it finds the target.\n\nExample 1:\n```\nvar v = new List<int> { 5, 8, 2, 9 };\nint target = 2;\nint idx = -1;\nfor (int i = 0; i < v.Count; i++) {\n    if (v[i] == target) {\n        idx = i;\n    }\n}\nConsole.WriteLine(idx);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n2\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\nvar v = new List<int> { 3, 6, 3, 8, 3 };\nint count = 0;\nforeach (int x in v) {\n    if (x == 3) {\n        count++;\n    }\n}\nConsole.WriteLine(count);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n3\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
