(834,19,13,'Type conversion','**Type conversion** changes a **value** from one **type** to another. Text must become a number before you can do math with it.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$t = "42";\n$n = (int)($t);\necho ($n + 8) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n50\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$n = 7;\n$s = strval($n);\necho ($s . " days") . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n7 days\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\n<?php\n$t = "8";\n$n = (int)($t);\necho ($n + 10) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n18\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(835,19,14,'Constants','A **constant** is a value that never changes, such as a tax rate. Write it in capital letters so it is easy to spot.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\nconst MAX = 5;\necho ("Max is ") . (MAX) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\nMax is 5\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\nconst PI = 3.14;\n$r = 2;\necho (PI * $r * $r) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n12.56\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(836,19,15,'Comments','A **comment** is a note for people. The computer **ignores** it. Use comments to explain what your code does.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n// This line is a comment\necho ("Hello") . PHP_EOL;\n// Comments help people read code\n```\nOutput:\n```\nHello\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n// Calculate the total\n$price = 20;\n$qty = 3;\necho ($price * $qty) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n60\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(837,19,16,'Naming rules','Give every **variable** a clear **name**. Use letters, digits and underscores, and never start with a digit. Capital letters matter.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$studentAge = 17;\n$total_score = 90;\necho ($studentAge + $total_score) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n107\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$firstName = "Ana";\necho ("Hello ") . ($firstName) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\nHello Ana\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\n<?php\n$studentAge = 7;\n$total_score = 5;\necho ($studentAge + $total_score) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n12\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(838,19,17,'Booleans','A **boolean** is either **true** or **false**. Use it for yes or no answers and for **conditions**.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$isOpen = true;\nif ($isOpen) {\n    echo ("Open") . PHP_EOL;\n} else {\n    echo ("Closed") . PHP_EOL;\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\nOpen\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$age = 20;\n$adult = $age >= 18;\nif ($adult) {\n    echo ("Adult") . PHP_EOL;\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\nAdult\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(839,19,18,'Increment and decrement','**++** adds 1 to a variable and **--** subtracts 1. They are handy for counting.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$n = 5;\n$n++;\necho ($n) . PHP_EOL;\n$n--;\necho ($n) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n6\n5\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$count = 0;\nfor ($i = 0; $i < 4; $i++) {\n    $count++;\n}\necho ($count) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n4\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(840,19,19,'Compound assignment','**Compound assignment** changes a variable using its own value: **+=**, **-=**, ***=** and **/=**.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$score = 10;\n$score += 5;\necho ($score) . PHP_EOL;\n$score *= 2;\necho ($score) . PHP_EOL;\n$score -= 3;\necho ($score) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n15\n30\n27\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$x = 20;\n$x /= 4;\necho ($x) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n5\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\n<?php\n$score = 10;\n$score += 8;\necho ($score) . PHP_EOL;\n$score *= 2;\necho ($score) . PHP_EOL;\n$score -= 3;\necho ($score) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n18\n36\n33\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(841,19,20,'Operator precedence','**Precedence** decides which **operator** runs first. Multiplication and division come before addition and subtraction. Use **brackets** to choose the order yourself.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\necho (2 + 3 * 4) . PHP_EOL;\necho ((2 + 3) * 4) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n14\n20\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\necho (10 - 4 - 3) . PHP_EOL;\necho (10 - (4 - 3)) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n3\n9\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\n<?php\necho (2 + 12 * 4) . PHP_EOL;\necho ((2 + 12) * 4) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n50\n56\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(842,19,21,'Nested if','A **nested if** is an if inside another if. The inner check only happens when the outer one is true.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$age = 20;\n$hasId = true;\nif ($age >= 18) {\n    if ($hasId) {\n        echo ("Welcome") . PHP_EOL;\n    } else {\n        echo ("Show your ID") . PHP_EOL;\n    }\n} else {\n    echo ("Too young") . PHP_EOL;\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\nWelcome\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$score = 85;\nif ($score >= 50) {\n    if ($score >= 80) {\n        echo ("Great") . PHP_EOL;\n    } else {\n        echo ("Pass") . PHP_EOL;\n    }\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\nGreat\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(843,19,22,'switch statements','A **switch** picks one **case** by matching a value. **break** stops it from running into the next case, and **default** handles everything else.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$day = 3;\nswitch ($day) {\n    case 1:\n        echo ("Monday") . PHP_EOL;\n        break;\n    case 3:\n        echo ("Wednesday") . PHP_EOL;\n        break;\n    default:\n        echo ("Another day") . PHP_EOL;\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\nWednesday\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$n = 2;\nswitch ($n) {\n    case 1:\n    case 2:\n        echo ("Small") . PHP_EOL;\n        break;\n    default:\n        echo ("Big") . PHP_EOL;\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\nSmall\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(844,19,23,'Ternary operator','The **ternary operator** is a short if: **condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse**.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$age = 17;\n$label = ($age >= 18) ? "adult" : "minor";\necho ($label) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\nminor\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$a = 8;\n$b = 5;\n$big = ($a > $b) ? $a : $b;\necho ($big) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n8\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(845,19,24,'do while loops','A **do while** loop runs its body **first** and checks the condition **after**, so it always runs at least once.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$n = 1;\ndo {\n    echo ($n) . PHP_EOL;\n    $n++;\n} while ($n <= 3);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n1\n2\n3\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$n = 10;\ndo {\n    echo ("Runs once") . PHP_EOL;\n    $n++;\n} while ($n < 5);\n```\nOutput:\n```\nRuns once\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\n<?php\n$n = 1;\ndo {\n    echo ($n) . PHP_EOL;\n    $n++;\n} while ($n <= 5);\n```\nOutput:\n```\n1\n2\n3\n4\n5\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(846,19,25,'break and continue','**break** leaves a loop right away. **continue** skips the rest of this round and goes to the next one.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\nfor ($i = 1; $i <= 6; $i++) {\n    if ($i == 3) {\n        continue;\n    }\n    if ($i == 5) {\n        break;\n    }\n    echo ($i) . PHP_EOL;\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n1\n2\n4\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$i = 0;\nwhile ($i < 10) {\n    $i += 3;\n    if ($i > 7) {\n        break;\n    }\n    echo ($i) . PHP_EOL;\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n3\n6\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\n<?php\nfor ($i = 1; $i <= 5; $i++) {\n    if ($i == 10) {\n        continue;\n    }\n    if ($i == 9) {\n        break;\n    }\n    echo ($i) . PHP_EOL;\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n1\n2\n3\n4\n5\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(847,19,26,'Nested loops','A **nested loop** is a loop inside a loop. The inner loop runs completely for every round of the outer loop.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\nfor ($r = 1; $r <= 3; $r++) {\n    for ($c = 1; $c <= 3; $c++) {\n        echo ($r * $c) . PHP_EOL;\n    }\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n1\n2\n3\n2\n4\n6\n3\n6\n9\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\nfor ($i = 1; $i <= 4; $i++) {\n    $line = "";\n    for ($j = 0; $j < $i; $j++) {\n        $line = $line . "*";\n    }\n    echo ($line) . PHP_EOL;\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n*\n**\n***\n****\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\n<?php\nfor ($r = 1; $r <= 5; $r++) {\n    for ($c = 1; $c <= 5; $c++) {\n        echo ($r * $c) . PHP_EOL;\n    }\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n1\n2\n3\n4\n5\n2\n4\n6\n8\n10\n3\n6\n9\n12\n15\n4\n8\n12\n16\n20\n5\n10\n15\n20\n25\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(848,19,27,'Counting with loops','Use a **counter** variable that starts at 0 and goes up by 1 each time something matches.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$count = 0;\nfor ($i = 1; $i <= 20; $i++) {\n    if ($i % 3 == 0) {\n        $count++;\n    }\n}\necho ($count) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n6\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$word = "banana";\n$a = 0;\nfor ($i = 0; $i < strlen($word); $i++) {\n    if ($word[$i] == \'a\') {\n        $a++;\n    }\n}\necho ($a) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n3\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\n<?php\n$count = 0;\nfor ($i = 1; $i <= 3; $i++) {\n    if ($i % 6 == 0) {\n        $count++;\n    }\n}\necho ($count) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n0\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(849,19,28,'Accumulator pattern','An **accumulator** is a variable that starts at 0 and **adds** to itself each round to build a total.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$total = 0;\nfor ($i = 1; $i <= 5; $i++) {\n    $total += $i;\n}\necho ($total) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n15\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$prices = [5, 10, 20];\n$sum = 0;\nforeach ($prices as $p) {\n    $sum += $p;\n}\necho ("Total: ") . ($sum) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\nTotal: 35\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\n<?php\n$total = 0;\nfor ($i = 1; $i <= 4; $i++) {\n    $total += $i;\n}\necho ($total) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n10\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(850,19,29,'Arrays basics','An **array** (or **list**) keeps many values under one name. Each value has a **position** called an **index**, and the first index is **0**.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$v = [4, 9, 2];\necho ($v[0]) . PHP_EOL;\necho ($v[2]) . PHP_EOL;\necho (count($v)) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n4\n2\n3\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$scores = [70, 85, 90];\nforeach ($scores as $s) {\n    echo ($s) . PHP_EOL;\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n70\n85\n90\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\n<?php\n$v = [5, 11, 2];\necho ($v[0]) . PHP_EOL;\necho ($v[2]) . PHP_EOL;\necho (count($v)) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n5\n2\n3\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(851,19,30,'Looping arrays','Use a **for** loop with the **index** to visit each item, or a loop that gives you each item directly.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$v = [3, 6, 9];\nfor ($i = 0; $i < count($v); $i++) {\n    echo ($i) . (": ") . ($v[$i]) . PHP_EOL;\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n0: 3\n1: 6\n2: 9\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$v = [2, 4, 6];\n$sum = 0;\nforeach ($v as $x) {\n    $sum += $x;\n}\necho ($sum) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n12\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\n<?php\n$v = [12, 4, 9];\nfor ($i = 0; $i < count($v); $i++) {\n    echo ($i) . (": ") . ($v[$i]) . PHP_EOL;\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n0: 12\n1: 4\n2: 9\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(852,19,31,'Array length','The **length** tells you how many items are in an array. The **last** index is always the length **minus 1**.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$v = [5, 6, 7, 8];\necho (count($v)) . PHP_EOL;\necho ($v[count($v) - 1]) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n4\n8\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$s = "hello";\necho (strlen($s)) . PHP_EOL;\necho ($s[strlen($s) - 1]) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n5\no\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(853,19,32,'Searching an array','To **search**, check every item and remember if you found what you want, or remember its **position**.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$v = [7, 3, 9, 4];\n$found = 0;\nforeach ($v as $x) {\n    if ($x == 9) {\n        $found = 1;\n    }\n}\nif ($found == 1) {\n    echo ("Found it") . PHP_EOL;\n} else {\n    echo ("Not found") . PHP_EOL;\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\nFound it\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$v = [7, 3, 9, 4];\n$pos = -1;\nfor ($i = 0; $i < count($v); $i++) {\n    if ($v[$i] == 3) {\n        $pos = $i;\n    }\n}\necho ($pos) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n1\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(854,19,33,'Finding min and max','Start with the **first** item as the biggest (or smallest) and **replace** it whenever you see a better one.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$v = [8, 2, 9, 4];\n$big = $v[0];\n$small = $v[0];\nforeach ($v as $x) {\n    if ($x > $big) {\n        $big = $x;\n    }\n    if ($x < $small) {\n        $small = $x;\n    }\n}\necho ("Biggest: ") . ($big) . PHP_EOL;\necho ("Smallest: ") . ($small) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\nBiggest: 9\nSmallest: 2\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$temps = [30, 28, 33];\n$hottest = $temps[0];\nforeach ($temps as $t) {\n    if ($t > $hottest) {\n        $hottest = $t;\n    }\n}\necho ($hottest) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n33\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(855,19,34,'String methods','A **string** is text. It has a **length**, and **methods** (built-in tools) such as taking a piece of it.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$s = "Hello World";\necho (strlen($s)) . PHP_EOL;\necho (substr($s, 0, 5)) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n11\nHello\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$a = "code";\n$b = "smart";\n$c = $a . $b;\necho ($c) . PHP_EOL;\necho (strlen($c)) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\ncodesmart\n9\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\n<?php\n$s = "Hello World";\necho (strlen($s)) . PHP_EOL;\necho (substr($s, 0, 7)) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n11\nHello W\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(856,19,35,'Substrings','A **substring** is a piece of a string. You give the **start** position and how many characters to take.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$s = "programming";\necho (substr($s, 0, 3)) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\npro\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$s = "programming";\necho (substr($s, 3, 4)) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\ngram\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\n<?php\n$s = "programming";\necho (substr($s, 0, 8)) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\nprogramm\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(857,19,36,'Searching text','You can **search** inside a string to see if a word is there and **where** it starts.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$s = "the cat sat";\nif (strpos($s, "cat") !== false) {\n    echo ("Found cat") . PHP_EOL;\n} else {\n    echo ("No cat") . PHP_EOL;\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\nFound cat\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$s = "the cat sat";\necho (strpos($s, "sat")) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n8\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(858,19,37,'Formatting output','Join text and values in one line to make clear **output**. An empty print makes a **blank line**.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$name = "Ana";\n$score = 95;\necho ($name) . (" scored ") . ($score) . (" points") . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\nAna scored 95 points\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\necho ("Line 1") . PHP_EOL;\necho ("") . PHP_EOL;\necho ("Line 3") . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\nLine 1\n\nLine 3\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\n<?php\n$name = "Ana";\n$score = 3;\necho ($name) . (" scored ") . ($score) . (" points") . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\nAna scored 3 points\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(859,19,38,'Function parameters','**Parameters** are the inputs of a **function**. The values you pass in when you call it are called **arguments**.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\nfunction add($a, $b) {\n    return $a + $b;\n}\n\necho (add(3, 4)) . PHP_EOL;\necho (add(10, 20)) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n7\n30\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\nfunction area($w, $h) {\n    return $w * $h;\n}\n\necho ("Area: ") . (area(4, 5)) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\nArea: 20\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\n<?php\nfunction add($a, $b) {\n    return $a + $b;\n}\n\necho (add(6, 3)) . PHP_EOL;\necho (add(9, 11)) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n9\n20\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(860,19,39,'Return values','**return** sends a **value** back to the place where the function was called. You can store it or use it right away.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\nfunction square($n) {\n    return $n * $n;\n}\n\n$r = square(6);\necho ($r) . PHP_EOL;\necho (square(3) + 1) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n36\n10\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\nfunction bigger($a, $b) {\n    if ($a > $b) {\n        return $a;\n    }\n    return $b;\n}\n\necho (bigger(7, 12)) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n12\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\n<?php\nfunction square($n) {\n    return $n * $n;\n}\n\n$r = square(10);\necho ($r) . PHP_EOL;\necho (square(9) + 1) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n100\n82\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(861,19,40,'Default parameters','A **default parameter** has a value that is used when the caller does not give one.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\nfunction power($base, $times = 2) {\n    $r = 1;\n    for ($i = 0; $i < $times; $i++) {\n        $r = $r * $base;\n    }\n    return $r;\n}\n\necho (power(5)) . PHP_EOL;\necho (power(2, 5)) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n25\n32\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\nfunction greet($name = "friend") {\n    return "Hello " . $name;\n}\n\necho (greet()) . PHP_EOL;\necho (greet("Ana")) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\nHello friend\nHello Ana\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.');
INSERT INTO `new_lessons`(id,module_id,position,title,body) VALUES
(862,19,41,'Function overview','A **function** is a named block of code you can **call** again and again. It keeps your program short and tidy.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\nfunction sayHi() {\n    echo ("Hi!") . PHP_EOL;\n}\n\nsayHi();\nsayHi();\n```\nOutput:\n```\nHi!\nHi!\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\nfunction line() {\n    echo ("----------") . PHP_EOL;\n}\n\nline();\necho ("Menu") . PHP_EOL;\nline();\n```\nOutput:\n```\n----------\nMenu\n----------\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(863,19,42,'Variable scope','**Scope** is where a variable can be used. A variable made inside a function or loop only exists **there**.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\nfunction show() {\n    $inner = 5;\n    echo ($inner) . PHP_EOL;\n}\n\n$outer = 1;\nshow();\necho ($outer) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n5\n1\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\nfor ($i = 0; $i < 2; $i++) {\n    $temp = $i * 10;\n    echo ($temp) . PHP_EOL;\n}\necho ("Done") . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n0\n10\nDone\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\n<?php\nfunction show() {\n    $inner = 3;\n    echo ($inner) . PHP_EOL;\n}\n\n$outer = 1;\nshow();\necho ($outer) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n3\n1\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(864,19,43,'Recursion intro','**Recursion** means a function **calls itself**. It needs a **base case** that stops it.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\nfunction countdownSum($n) {\n    if ($n == 0) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    return $n + countdownSum($n - 1);\n}\n\necho (countdownSum(4)) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n10\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\nfunction fact($n) {\n    if ($n <= 1) {\n        return 1;\n    }\n    return $n * fact($n - 1);\n}\n\necho (fact(5)) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n120\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\n<?php\nfunction countdownSum($n) {\n    if ($n == 0) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    return $n + countdownSum($n - 1);\n}\n\necho (countdownSum(6)) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n21\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(865,19,44,'Simple calculator','A **calculator** uses the math **operators** on two numbers. Whole-number division drops the decimals.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$a = 12;\n$b = 4;\necho ($a + $b) . PHP_EOL;\necho ($a - $b) . PHP_EOL;\necho ($a * $b) . PHP_EOL;\necho (intdiv($a, $b)) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n16\n8\n48\n3\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$x = 9;\n$y = 3;\n$op = 3;\nswitch ($op) {\n    case 1:\n        echo ($x + $y) . PHP_EOL;\n        break;\n    case 3:\n        echo ($x * $y) . PHP_EOL;\n        break;\n    default:\n        echo ("Unknown") . PHP_EOL;\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n27\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\n<?php\n$a = 4;\n$b = 9;\necho ($a + $b) . PHP_EOL;\necho ($a - $b) . PHP_EOL;\necho ($a * $b) . PHP_EOL;\necho (intdiv($a, $b)) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n13\n-5\n36\n0\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(866,19,45,'Number guessing logic','A guessing game **compares** the guess with a secret number and tells the player **too low**, **too high** or **correct**.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$secret = 7;\n$guess = 4;\nif ($guess == $secret) {\n    echo ("Correct") . PHP_EOL;\n} else if ($guess < $secret) {\n    echo ("Too low") . PHP_EOL;\n} else {\n    echo ("Too high") . PHP_EOL;\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\nToo low\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$guesses = [3, 9, 7];\n$secret = 7;\nforeach ($guesses as $g) {\n    if ($g == $secret) {\n        echo ("Got it: ") . ($g) . PHP_EOL;\n    } else {\n        echo ("Try again") . PHP_EOL;\n    }\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\nTry again\nTry again\nGot it: 7\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\n<?php\n$secret = 3;\n$guess = 12;\nif ($guess == $secret) {\n    echo ("Correct") . PHP_EOL;\n} else if ($guess < $secret) {\n    echo ("Too low") . PHP_EOL;\n} else {\n    echo ("Too high") . PHP_EOL;\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\nToo high\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(867,19,46,'Temperature converter','To change **Celsius** to **Fahrenheit**, multiply by 9, divide by 5 and add 32.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$c = 20;\n$f = $c * 9 / 5 + 32;\necho ($f) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n68\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\nfor ($c = 0; $c <= 40; $c += 20) {\n    echo ($c) . (" C = ") . ($c * 9 / 5 + 32) . (" F") . PHP_EOL;\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n0 C = 32 F\n20 C = 68 F\n40 C = 104 F\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(868,19,47,'Grade calculator','A **grade calculator** uses **if / else if** to turn a score into a letter. Check the highest score first.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$score = 86;\nif ($score >= 90) {\n    echo ("A") . PHP_EOL;\n} else if ($score >= 80) {\n    echo ("B") . PHP_EOL;\n} else if ($score >= 70) {\n    echo ("C") . PHP_EOL;\n} else {\n    echo ("F") . PHP_EOL;\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\nB\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$marks = [80, 90, 70];\n$sum = 0;\nforeach ($marks as $m) {\n    $sum += $m;\n}\necho ("Average: ") . (intdiv($sum, count($marks))) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\nAverage: 80\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(869,19,48,'Reading error messages','An **error message** tells you what went wrong and **which line**. Read the first message first, fix it, then run again. Typical errors are a **missing semicolon**, a **misspelled name** and **mixed types**.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$count = 3;\necho ("Count is ") . ($count) . PHP_EOL;\n// A missing semicolon on the line above would give an error\n```\nOutput:\n```\nCount is 3\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$name = "Ana";\necho ("Hello ") . ($name) . PHP_EOL;\n// Writing nam instead of name would give an unknown name error\n```\nOutput:\n```\nHello Ana\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(870,19,49,'Debugging basics','**Debugging** means finding and fixing mistakes. A great trick is to **print** the values while the program runs.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$total = 0;\nfor ($i = 1; $i <= 3; $i++) {\n    $total += $i;\n    echo ("i = ") . ($i) . (" total = ") . ($total) . PHP_EOL;\n}\necho ($total) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\ni = 1 total = 1\ni = 2 total = 3\ni = 3 total = 6\n6\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$a = 6;\n$b = 4;\necho ("a is ") . ($a) . PHP_EOL;\necho ("b is ") . ($b) . PHP_EOL;\necho ("a - b is ") . ($a - $b) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\na is 6\nb is 4\na - b is 2\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\n<?php\n$total = 0;\nfor ($i = 1; $i <= 4; $i++) {\n    $total += $i;\n    echo ("i = ") . ($i) . (" total = ") . ($total) . PHP_EOL;\n}\necho ($total) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\ni = 1 total = 1\ni = 2 total = 3\ni = 3 total = 6\ni = 4 total = 10\n10\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(871,19,50,'Mini project','Time to combine what you learned: **variables**, **loops**, **if** and **arrays** in small programs.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n// Mini project: multiplication table\n$n = 4;\nfor ($i = 1; $i <= 5; $i++) {\n    echo ($n) . (" x ") . ($i) . (" = ") . ($n * $i) . PHP_EOL;\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n4 x 1 = 4\n4 x 2 = 8\n4 x 3 = 12\n4 x 4 = 16\n4 x 5 = 20\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$v = [5, 12, 8];\n$sum = 0;\n$big = $v[0];\nforeach ($v as $x) {\n    $sum += $x;\n    if ($x > $big) {\n        $big = $x;\n    }\n}\necho ("Sum: ") . ($sum) . PHP_EOL;\necho ("Biggest: ") . ($big) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\nSum: 25\nBiggest: 12\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\n<?php\n// Mini project: multiplication table\n$n = 12;\nfor ($i = 1; $i <= 6; $i++) {\n    echo ($n) . (" x ") . ($i) . (" = ") . ($n * $i) . PHP_EOL;\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n12 x 1 = 12\n12 x 2 = 24\n12 x 3 = 36\n12 x 4 = 48\n12 x 5 = 60\n12 x 6 = 72\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(872,26,1,'Functions in depth','A **function** can take several **parameters**, return a **value** and call other functions. Split a big problem into small functions.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\nfunction square($n) {\n    return $n * $n;\n}\nfunction sumOfSquares($a, $b) {\n    return square($a) + square($b);\n}\n\necho (sumOfSquares(3, 4)) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n25\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\nfunction isEven($n) {\n    if ($n % 2 == 0) {\n        return 1;\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n\necho (isEven(8)) . PHP_EOL;\necho (isEven(7)) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n1\n0\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\n<?php\nfunction square($n) {\n    return $n * $n;\n}\nfunction sumOfSquares($a, $b) {\n    return square($a) + square($b);\n}\n\necho (sumOfSquares(6, 8)) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n100\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(873,26,2,'References in PHP',NULL),
(874,26,3,'Recursion','**Recursion** solves a big problem by solving a smaller copy of it. Always write the **base case** first.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\nfunction power($b, $e) {\n    if ($e == 0) {\n        return 1;\n    }\n    return $b * power($b, $e - 1);\n}\n\necho (power(2, 5)) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n32\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\nfunction sumDigits($n) {\n    if ($n < 10) {\n        return $n;\n    }\n    return $n % 10 + sumDigits(intdiv($n, 10));\n}\n\necho (sumDigits(1234)) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n10\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\n<?php\nfunction power($b, $e) {\n    if ($e == 0) {\n        return 1;\n    }\n    return $b * power($b, $e - 1);\n}\n\necho (power(2, 3)) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n8\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(875,26,4,'Scope in depth',NULL),
(876,26,5,'Strings in depth','You can walk through a **string** one **character** at a time with its **index**, to reverse it or count things.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$s = "hello";\n$r = "";\nfor ($i = 0; $i < strlen($s); $i++) {\n    $r = $s[$i] . $r;\n}\necho ($r) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\nolleh\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$s = "education";\n$c = 0;\nfor ($i = 0; $i < strlen($s); $i++) {\n    if ($s[$i] == \'a\' || $s[$i] == \'e\' || $s[$i] == \'i\' || $s[$i] == \'o\' || $s[$i] == \'u\') {\n        $c++;\n    }\n}\necho ($c) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n5\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(877,26,6,'String formatting',NULL),
(878,26,7,'Splitting text',NULL),
(879,26,8,'Searching and replacing',NULL),
(880,26,9,'Arrays in depth','You can **change** the items of an array while you loop over it, and use **nested** loops to compare items.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$v = [1, 2, 3, 4];\nfor ($i = 0; $i < count($v); $i++) {\n    $v[$i] = $v[$i] * 2;\n}\nforeach ($v as $x) {\n    echo ($x) . PHP_EOL;\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n2\n4\n6\n8\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$v = [4, 7, 4, 9];\n$dups = 0;\nfor ($i = 0; $i < count($v); $i++) {\n    for ($j = $i + 1; $j < count($v); $j++) {\n        if ($v[$i] == $v[$j]) {\n            $dups++;\n        }\n    }\n}\necho ($dups) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n1\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\n<?php\n$v = [1, 2, 4, 5];\nfor ($i = 0; $i < count($v); $i++) {\n    $v[$i] = $v[$i] * 2;\n}\nforeach ($v as $x) {\n    echo ($x) . PHP_EOL;\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n2\n4\n8\n10\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(881,26,10,'Two-dimensional arrays',NULL),
(882,26,11,'Sorting a list','**Sorting** puts items in order. **Bubble sort** compares neighbors and swaps them when they are in the wrong order.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$v = [9, 3, 7, 1];\n$n = count($v);\nfor ($i = 0; $i < $n - 1; $i++) {\n    for ($j = 0; $j < $n - 1 - $i; $j++) {\n        if ($v[$j] > $v[$j + 1]) {\n            $t = $v[$j];\n            $v[$j] = $v[$j + 1];\n            $v[$j + 1] = $t;\n        }\n    }\n}\nforeach ($v as $x) {\n    echo ($x) . PHP_EOL;\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n1\n3\n7\n9\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$v = [4, 8, 2, 6];\n$n = count($v);\nfor ($i = 0; $i < $n - 1; $i++) {\n    for ($j = 0; $j < $n - 1 - $i; $j++) {\n        if ($v[$j] < $v[$j + 1]) {\n            $t = $v[$j];\n            $v[$j] = $v[$j + 1];\n            $v[$j + 1] = $t;\n        }\n    }\n}\nforeach ($v as $x) {\n    echo ($x) . PHP_EOL;\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n8\n6\n4\n2\n```\n\nExample 3:\n```\n<?php\n$v = [4, 11, 3, 1];\n$n = count($v);\nfor ($i = 0; $i < $n - 1; $i++) {\n    for ($j = 0; $j < $n - 1 - $i; $j++) {\n        if ($v[$j] > $v[$j + 1]) {\n            $t = $v[$j];\n            $v[$j] = $v[$j + 1];\n            $v[$j + 1] = $t;\n        }\n    }\n}\nforeach ($v as $x) {\n    echo ($x) . PHP_EOL;\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```\n1\n3\n4\n11\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
(883,26,12,'Searching a list','**Linear search** checks the items one by one from the start until it finds the target.\n\nExample 1:\n```\n<?php\n$v = [5, 8, 2, 9];\n$target = 2;\n$idx = -1;\nfor ($i = 0; $i < count($v); $i++) {\n    if ($v[$i] == $target) {\n        $idx = $i;\n    }\n}\necho ($idx) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n2\n```\n\nExample 2:\n```\n<?php\n$v = [3, 6, 3, 8, 3];\n$count = 0;\nforeach ($v as $x) {\n    if ($x == 3) {\n        $count++;\n    }\n}\necho ($count) . PHP_EOL;\n```\nOutput:\n```\n3\n```\n\nPractice: change the numbers or words in Example 1, then guess the new output before you run it.'),
